 String
Definition
String is an immutable class in Java used to represent a sequence of characters.

Key Points
Immutable → cannot be changed
Stored in String Constant Pool (SCP)
Thread-safe (because immutable)

Example
String s = "Java";
s.concat(" World");
System.out.println(s);

Output
Java

🔹 StringBuilder
Definition

StringBuilder is a mutable, non-thread-safe class used to create dynamic strings.

Key Points
Faster than StringBuffer
Introduced in Java 5
Not synchronized

Example
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Java");
sb.append(" World");
System.out.println(sb);

Output
Java World

🔹 StringBuffer
Definition
StringBuffer is a mutable, thread-safe class used for string manipulation.

Key Points
Synchronized
Slower than StringBuilder
Used in multithreaded environments

Example
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Java");
sb.append(" World");
System.out.println(sb);

Output
Java World

🔹 String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer
| Feature     | String      | StringBuilder | StringBuffer |
| ----------- | ----------- | ------------- | ------------ |
| Mutability  | ❌ Immutable | ✅ Mutable     | ✅ Mutable    |
| Thread-safe | ✅           | ❌             | ✅            |
| Performance | Slow        | Fastest       | Slow         |
| Introduced  | Java 1.0    | Java 5        | Java 1.0     |

  Q1. Why String is immutable?

A: For security, caching, thread safety, and memory optimization.

Q2. Difference between == and equals() in String?
A:
== compares references
equals() compares content

🔥 Tricky Question
String s1 = "Java";
String s2 = new String("Java");
System.out.println(s1 == s2);

Output: false
