🔹 What is Exception Handling?

Exception Handling is a mechanism to handle runtime errors so that the normal flow of the program is not disrupted.

An Exception is an unwanted or unexpected event that occurs during program execution.

🔹 Exception Hierarchy
Object
 └── Throwable
      ├── Error
      │     └── OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError
      └── Exception
            ├── Checked Exceptions
            │     └── IOException, SQLException
            └── RuntimeException
                  └── NullPointerException, ArithmeticException

Error
Serious problems
Not recoverable
Not handled by developers

Exception
Recoverable
Handled using try-catch

🔹 Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions
| Feature                 | Checked             | Unchecked                  |
| ----------------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Checked at compile time | ✅                   | ❌                          |
| Must be handled         | ✅                   | ❌                          |
| Package                 | java.lang.Exception | java.lang.RuntimeException |
| Example                 | IOException         | NullPointerException       |

  Example
// Checked Exception
FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt"); // Compile-time error

// Unchecked Exception
int a = 10 / 0; // ArithmeticException

🔹 try-catch-finally
Definition

try → risky code

catch → handles exception

finally → executes always (cleanup)

Example
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Exception caught");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Finally block");
}

Output
Exception caught
Finally block

🔹 throw vs throws
| throw                              | throws                    |
| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Used to explicitly throw exception | Used to declare exception |
| Inside method                      | Method signature          |
| Throws single exception            | Can declare multiple      |
  
Example
void validate(int age) {
    if(age < 18)
        throw new ArithmeticException("Not eligible");
}

void readFile() throws IOException {
    FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt");
}

🔹 Custom Exceptions
Definition

User-defined exceptions created by extending Exception or RuntimeException.

Example
class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {
    InvalidAgeException(String msg) {
        super(msg);
    }
}

class Test {
    static void validate(int age) throws InvalidAgeException {
        if(age < 18)
            throw new InvalidAgeException("Age below 18");
    }
}

🔹 try-with-resources (Java 7+)
Definition

Automatically closes resources that implement AutoCloseable.

Example
try (FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt")) {
    System.out.println("Reading file");
}

✔ No need for finally
✔ Prevents memory leaks
 
